@using WorriorVex.Application.Platform
@inject AppInfo Info
@inject IPlatformShell Shell

@* English documentation. The other languages are DocsNl, DocsDe and DocsPl; keep the topics in step. *@
@switch (Topic)
{
    case "start":
        <h2>Getting started</h2>
        <p>
            WorriorVex is a notebook that lives on your computer. Everything you write is saved
            here, on this machine. It works with the internet switched off and needs no account.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>Press <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>N</kbd>, or choose <strong>+ New</strong>, to start a note.</li>
            <li>Type a title, press <kbd>Enter</kbd>, and write.</li>
            <li>That is all. The note is saved for you, and it is there when you come back.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            New notes go to the <strong>Inbox</strong> unless you are inside a notebook or folder.
            Capture first; decide where it belongs later.
        </p>
        <h3>The window</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Left:</strong> where things are. Drag its right edge to make it wider; <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>\</kbd> hides or shows it. Inbox, All Notes, your notebooks and their folders, the Trash.</li>
            <li><strong>Middle:</strong> the notes in the place you selected, in the order you added them (a new note joins at the bottom). Its edge can be dragged too.</li>
            <li><strong>Right:</strong> the note you are working on.</li>
            <li><strong>Bottom:</strong> whether your work is saved.</li>
        </ul>
        break;

    case "writing":
        <h2>Writing notes</h2>
        <p>
            The toolbar above the text gives you bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, inline code,
            three levels of heading, bulleted, numbered and check lists, quotes, code blocks and a
            dividing line. Most have a keyboard shortcut, listed under <em>Keyboard shortcuts</em>.
        </p>
        <h3>Links</h3>
        <p>
            Select some words and choose <strong>Link</strong>, then type the web address. With nothing
            selected, the address itself is inserted as the link. To change a link, put the cursor in it
            and choose <strong>Link</strong> again; <strong>Unlink</strong> removes it.
            Hold <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> and click a link to open it in your browser.
        </p>
        <h3>Links between notes</h3>
        <p>
            Choose <strong>Note link</strong>, find the note you mean and pick it. With text selected, that
            text becomes the link; otherwise the other note's title is inserted. Hold <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> and
            click such a link to jump to that note.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under the text, <strong>Links to</strong> lists the notes this one links to, <strong>Linked from</strong>
            the notes that link here (backlinks), and <strong>Related by tags</strong> notes that share tags with
            this one. They are kept up to date as you write; nothing needs to be maintained by hand.
            The words of a link do not change when the other note is renamed.
        </p>
        <h3>Images</h3>
        <p>
            Choose <strong>Image</strong> to pick a picture from your computer, paste one from the
            clipboard, or drag an image file onto the note. PNG, JPEG, GIF and WebP are accepted.
            The picture is copied into WorriorVex's own data folder, so the note keeps working if the
            original file is moved or deleted.
        </p>
        <p>
            Pictures that live on the internet are not shown: text pasted from a web page keeps its
            words and loses its pictures. This is deliberate, so that opening a note never contacts
            another computer.
        </p>
        <p>Point at an image and drag the small square at its bottom-right corner to make it smaller or larger.</p>
        <h3>Attached files</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Attach</strong> adds any file to the note, as does dropping a file that is not a picture onto
            the text. Attachments are listed under the note with <strong>Open</strong> (in the program your
            system uses for that kind of file), <strong>Save as…</strong> (a copy wherever you choose),
            <strong>Rename…</strong> and <strong>Remove…</strong>. The file is copied into WorriorVex's data
            folder when attached; the original is never touched.
        </p>
        <p>
            Pictures you placed in the text are attachments too. One that no longer appears in the text is
            marked "not in the text", and a button offers to remove all such leftovers at once.
        </p>
        <h3>Tables</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Table</strong> inserts a table of three columns with a header row. While the cursor
            is in a table, buttons appear to add or delete rows and columns, to switch the header row
            on or off, to merge selected cells or split one, and to delete the table. <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves to
            the next cell and adds a row at the end. Drag the line between two columns to change their widths.
        </p>
        <h3>Marker, callouts and code</h3>
        <p>
            The <mark>ab</mark> button marks the selected words as with a highlighter. <strong>Callout…</strong>
            puts the current paragraph in a coloured box (Info, Tip, Warning or Danger); choose it again to
            take the box away. A code block gets a <strong>Language</strong> list while the cursor is in it;
            pick one and the code is coloured.
        </p>
        <h3>Find and replace</h3>
        <p>
            <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>F</kbd> (or <strong>Find</strong> on the toolbar) opens a bar above the
            text. Matches are marked as you type; <kbd>Enter</kbd> goes to the next, <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd>
            to the previous. <strong>Replace</strong> changes the current match, <strong>All</strong> every match.
            <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes the bar.
        </p>
        <h3>Templates</h3>
        <p>
            <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>N</kbd>, or the <strong>▾</strong> next to <strong>+ New</strong>,
            makes a note from a template. Templates are ordinary notes in a notebook called <strong>Templates</strong>,
            created with three starters (meeting notes, a daily note, a project) the first time you use it. Edit
            them or add your own; write <code>{{date}}</code> where today's date should appear.
        </p>
        <h3>Typing shortcuts</h3>
        <p>At the start of a line, type one of these followed by a space:</p>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">To get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>#</code>, <code>##</code>, <code>###</code></td><td>A heading</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>-</code> or <code>*</code></td><td>A bulleted list</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>1.</code></td><td>A numbered list</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>[ ]</code></td><td>A check list</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&gt;</code></td><td>A quote</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>```</code></td><td>A code block</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>[[</code> (anywhere)</td><td>A link to another note: a picker opens</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3>Saving</h3>
        <p>
            Notes save themselves a moment after you stop typing. The bar at the bottom shows
            <strong>Saved</strong>, <strong>Unsaved changes</strong> or <strong>Saving</strong>.
            Press <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd> to save at once.
        </p>
        <p>
            If a save fails, the bar says so and offers <strong>Retry</strong>. Your text stays in the
            window, and WorriorVex will not let you move to another note until it is safely stored.
        </p>
        <h3>Earlier versions</h3>
        <p>
            While you edit, WorriorVex quietly keeps the version from before a change about every ten
            minutes. <strong>History</strong> in the toolbar lists them; select one to read it and
            <strong>Restore this version</strong> to bring it back. What the note says at that moment is kept
            as a version too, so restoring never loses anything.
        </p>
        break;

    case "searching":
        <h2>Searching</h2>
        <p>
            The search box at the top looks through the titles, text and tags of all your notes.
            Press <kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>K</kbd> to jump to it and start typing; results appear as
            you type, best matches first, with the matching words marked. A match in the title counts
            more than one in the text.
        </p>
        <p>
            When you start a search from a notebook or folder, tick <strong>Only in “…”</strong> above the
            results to look there alone. <strong>Save search</strong> keeps the search in the navigation pane
            under <strong>Saved searches</strong> (right-click one to rename or remove it). If a word finds
            nothing but a word in your notes is close to it, the results offer <em>Did you mean …?</em>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Every word you type must occur. A word also matches longer words that start with it: <code>archi</code> finds <em>architecture</em>.</li>
            <li>Accents and case do not matter: <code>resume</code> finds <em>Résumé</em>.</li>
            <li>Put an exact phrase in quotes: <code>"quarterly plan"</code>.</li>
            <li><code>tag:project</code> finds notes carrying that tag.</li>
            <li><code>in:Projects</code> limits the results to a notebook or folder of that name; use quotes for names with spaces.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Use the arrow keys to move through the results and <kbd>Enter</kbd> to open one, or click it.
            <kbd>Esc</kbd> leaves the search and takes you back to where you were. Notes in the Trash are not searched.
        </p>
        break;

    case "organising":
        <h2>Notebooks and folders</h2>
        <p>
            A <strong>notebook</strong> is a top-level container, such as <em>Projects</em> or
            <em>Personal</em>. Inside a notebook you can make <strong>folders</strong>, and folders inside folders.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>New notebook:</strong> the <strong>+</strong> beside <em>Notebooks</em>.</li>
            <li><strong>New note or folder inside:</strong> point at a notebook or folder in the navigation and press the <strong>+</strong> at the end of its row, then choose <strong>New note</strong> or <strong>New folder</strong>. A place can hold as many notes and folders as you like, and folders nest as deep as you like. Selecting a note in the tree also selects its folder, so <strong>+ New</strong> adds the next note beside it.</li>
            <li><strong>Adding under a note:</strong> a note cannot hold other notes, but its row has a <strong>+</strong> too: it turns the note into a folder of the same name with the note kept inside, and adds your new note or folder there.</li>
            <li><strong>Collapse and expand:</strong> the small <strong>−</strong> / <strong>+</strong> box at the left of a row. Notes appear as leaves under their folder (switch this off in Settings → Appearance if you prefer folders only).</li>
            <li><strong>Rename:</strong> double-click a notebook, folder or note, or press <kbd>F2</kbd> on it; <kbd>Enter</kbd> keeps the new name, <kbd>Esc</kbd> gives up.</li>
            <li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> move through the tree, <kbd>→</kbd> opens a branch, <kbd>←</kbd> closes it or steps out, <kbd>Delete</kbd> moves the row to the trash.</li>
            <li><strong>Order of notes:</strong> the list above the notes shows them in the order added (new ones at the bottom), or by last change, newest first or title; pinned notes always stay on top. Settings → Appearance has a compact mode for long lists.</li>
            <li><strong>Rename or delete a folder or notebook:</strong> select it, then <strong>Rename folder…</strong> / <strong>Delete folder…</strong> (or <em>notebook…</em>) above the note list. Deleting a folder or notebook takes everything inside it to the trash; the confirmation says how much. A single note is deleted with <strong>Delete note</strong> above its text.</li>
            <li><strong>New note in a folder:</strong> select the folder, then <strong>+ New</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <strong>All Notes</strong> shows every note from every notebook in one list, with the notebook's name under each title.
            The Inbox is always there and cannot be renamed or deleted.
        </p>
        <h3>Moving things</h3>
        <p>
            Drag a note from the list, or a folder from the tree, and drop it on a notebook or folder.
            Or right-click it and choose <strong>Move…</strong> to pick the destination from a list.
            A folder takes everything inside it along. To change the order instead, drop a folder or notebook on the top or bottom edge of another row: a line shows where it will go, and it lands before or after that row. Right-click offers <strong>Move up</strong> and <strong>Move down</strong> too. A whole notebook can be dragged onto another notebook or a folder too: after you confirm, it becomes a folder there with everything it held.
        </p>
        <h3>Right-click menus</h3>
        <p>
            Right-click a notebook, folder, tag or note for what you can do with it: new note or folder,
            rename, move, duplicate, favourite, pin, delete. <strong>Duplicate</strong> makes a copy beside
            the original, with its tags and its own copies of its pictures.
        </p>
        <h3>Favourites and pins</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Favorite</strong> (★ above the note) makes a note easy to reach from the
            <strong>Favorites</strong> list, wherever it is filed. <strong>Pin</strong> keeps a note at the
            top of the list it lives in. They are separate: a note can be either, both or neither.
        </p>
        <h3>Tags</h3>
        <p>
            Type a tag in the row under the title and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>; existing tags are suggested.
            A tag is one word: no spaces or commas. Tags appear in the navigation with the number of notes
            carrying them; select one to see those notes, right-click it to rename or delete it. Deleting a
            tag never deletes a note. Search for <code>tag:name</code> to combine a tag with other words.
        </p>
        <h3>Loose ends</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Loose ends</strong> lists the notes that have no tag and no link to or from another note:
            a quick way to find what still needs filing. In every list, ⛓ shows how many links a note has.
        </p>
        <h3>Calendar</h3>
        <p>
            The <strong>Calendar</strong> heading in the navigation has <strong>Month view</strong>, a month at a time, and <strong>All calendar notes</strong>, the same notes as a list. Click a day and type a short note;
            <kbd>Enter</kbd> keeps it and starts the next, so a day can hold as many as you like. Click a note to change
            its words, <strong>↗</strong> to open it in the editor and write more (a trailing … marks notes that have more
            inside), <strong>×</strong> to move it to the trash. <strong>‹</strong> <strong>›</strong> change the month and
            <strong>Today</strong> comes back. Calendar notes are ordinary notes kept in a built-in notebook of their own: they are found by search and can carry tags and links. A small dot on a day means
            other notes were changed that day.
        </p>
        <h3>Recent</h3>
        <p><strong>Recent</strong> lists the notes you opened most recently. Opening a note does not count as changing it.</p>
        break;

    case "trash":
        <h2>The Trash</h2>
        <p>
            Deleting never destroys anything straight away. A deleted note, folder or notebook moves
            to the <strong>Trash</strong> and stays there until you decide.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Delete a note:</strong> <strong>Delete note</strong> at the right above the text.</li>
            <li><strong>Delete a folder or notebook:</strong> select it, then <strong>Delete folder…</strong> or <strong>Delete notebook…</strong> above the note list. Everything inside goes with it, and comes back with it when restored.</li>
            <li><strong>Restore:</strong> open the Trash, select the item, choose <strong>Restore</strong>. A folder or notebook comes back with everything that was in it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            If the place an item came from no longer exists, it is restored to the top of its
            notebook, or to the Inbox when the notebook is gone too. WorriorVex tells you when it does this.
        </p>
        <h3>Removing for good</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Delete permanently</strong> and <strong>Empty trash</strong> remove notes together with
            their earlier versions and attached files. This cannot be undone, so you are always asked first.
        </p>
        break;

    case "shortcuts":
        <h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Press</th><th scope="col">To</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>N</kbd></td><td>Start a new note</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>N</kbd></td><td>New note from a template</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>F</kbd></td><td>Find and replace in the note</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>F</kbd></td><td>Focus mode: only the note (again to leave)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Save now</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>K</kbd></td><td>Search</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>\</kbd></td><td>Hide or show the sidebar</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> <kbd>Enter</kbd> in the search box</td><td>Move through the results and open one</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd> in the search box</td><td>Leave the search</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd> in the title</td><td>Move to the text</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Close a dialog or menu</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd> in the tag row</td><td>Add the tag</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Backspace</kbd> in the empty tag row</td><td>Remove the last tag</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>B</kbd> / <kbd>I</kbd> / <kbd>U</kbd></td><td>Bold / italic / underline</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Strikethrough</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>E</kbd></td><td>Inline code</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>1</kbd>–<kbd>3</kbd></td><td>Heading 1–3</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>7</kbd> / <kbd>8</kbd> / <kbd>9</kbd></td><td>Numbered / bulleted / check list</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>B</kbd></td><td>Quote</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd></td><td>Code block</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Tab</kbd> / <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Tab</kbd> in a list</td><td>Indent / outdent</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>Tab</kbd> / <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Tab</kbd> in a table</td><td>Next / previous cell</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + click on a link</td><td>Open it: a web page in your browser, a note in WorriorVex</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>V</kbd> with a picture copied</td><td>Paste the picture into the note</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd></td><td>Undo</td></tr>
                <tr><td><kbd>@Modifier</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd></td><td>Redo</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        break;

    case "import":
        <h2>Importing</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Import &amp; backup → Import</strong> brings notes in from two other places.
            A <strong>WorriorVex package</strong> (<code>.worriorvex</code>, made with Export → "Everything, as a
            package") from another computer is added as new notebooks; links, tags and files come along,
            and nothing you already have is changed. A <strong>folder of Markdown or text files</strong>
            (an Obsidian vault, a Joplin export, your own <code>.md</code> files) becomes one notebook:
            subfolders turn into folders, pictures are attached, <code>[[wiki links]]</code> and relative
            links become links between notes, and tags in front matter or written as <code>#tag</code> are kept.
        </p>
        <h3>Checking attached files</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Check attached files</strong> compares the attachments your notes list with the files on
            disk and reports files that are missing, files that changed outside WorriorVex, and files nothing
            refers to any more (which it can delete for you).
        </p>
        <h2>Importing from KeepNote</h2>
        <p>
            Choose <strong>Import &amp; backup</strong> in the navigation, then <strong>Choose a KeepNote notebook
            folder…</strong> and pick the notebook's own folder: the one that contains <code>notebook.nbk</code>.
            WorriorVex first reads the notebook and shows what it found: pages, folders, files, pictures, links and
            the KeepNote trash. Nothing is changed until you choose <strong>Import</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>The notebook becomes a new WorriorVex notebook with the same name. Folders stay folders; pages become notes.</li>
            <li>A page that has pages inside it becomes a folder of the same name, with the page's own text as the first note in that folder.</li>
            <li>Files attached to a page become attachments of the note; pictures in the page are placed in the note's text.</li>
            <li>Links between pages become links between notes. Links to pages that no longer exist keep their words only.</li>
            <li>What was in KeepNote's Trash goes into the WorriorVex Trash.</li>
            <li>Created and modified times are kept. KeepNote settings such as icons and colours have no place here and are listed as ignored.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The KeepNote folder is only read; it is never changed, so you can keep using KeepNote or import again.
            Notebooks in KeepNote format version 5 or 6 (KeepNote 0.7) are supported; for an older notebook, open it
            once in KeepNote, which upgrades it, and import again. The report at the end says exactly what happened.
        </p>
        break;

    case "settings":
        <h2>Settings</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Settings</strong> in the navigation. Changes apply at once.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Theme:</strong> follow the system, always light or dark, or one of two high-contrast themes.</li>
            <li><strong>Accent colour</strong> for selections and buttons; <strong>Font</strong> for the note text (system, serif, sans-serif or monospace).</li>
            <li><strong>Lists:</strong> comfortable or compact rows, and whether notes appear in the navigation tree.</li>
            <li><strong>Text size and line height</strong> of the note text; <strong>spell checking</strong> on or off.</li>
            <li><strong>Save after a pause of:</strong> how long after your last keystroke a note is saved.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask before moving a note to the trash</strong>, for the cautious.</li>
            <li><strong>Open where I left off:</strong> start in the notebook or folder you had open, instead of the Inbox.</li>
            <li><strong>Check for a new version when WorriorVex starts:</strong> off by default; see <em>Your data → Privacy</em>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The settings are kept in <code>settings.json</code> in your data folder. Deleting that file brings the defaults back.</p>
        break;

    case "data":
        <h2>Your data</h2>
        <p>Your notes are yours. They are kept in one folder on this computer:</p>
        <p>
            <code>@Info.DataDirectory</code>
            <button type="button" class="wn-button wn-button-small" @onclick="() => Shell.OpenFolder(Info.DataDirectory)">Open folder</button>
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">In that folder</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>worriorvex.db</code></td><td>All notes, notebooks and folders, in a SQLite database</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>attachments</code></td><td>The pictures in your notes and the files attached to them</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>settings.json</code></td><td>Your settings</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>backups</code></td><td>Backups you made, and the safety backups taken before each restore</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>logs</code></td><td>A daily log, useful when reporting a problem. It never contains what you wrote</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3>Backing up</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Import &amp; backup → Back up now</strong> writes one zip file with your whole database, every
            attached file and a description of what is inside, into the <code>backups</code> folder. <strong>Save a
            backup to…</strong> puts it where you choose, for example on another disk. Backups are safe to take
            while you work.
        </p>
        <h3>Automatic backups</h3>
        <p>
            On the same page you can have WorriorVex take a backup every day or every week while it is open.
            These go to the backups folder with names starting <code>auto-</code>, and only the newest few
            (you choose how many) are kept. Backups you take yourself are never deleted.
        </p>
        <h3>Restoring</h3>
        <p>
            Choose <strong>Restore…</strong> beside a backup, or <strong>Restore from a file…</strong> for one kept
            elsewhere. WorriorVex checks the file first and refuses one that is damaged or from a newer version.
            Before anything is replaced, what you have now is written to a new backup, so a restore can always be
            undone by restoring that one.
        </p>
        <h3>Exporting</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>Export to a folder…</strong> writes every note as a file, in the notebook and folder structure,
            as HTML (self-contained pages with pictures embedded), Markdown (with a folder of pictures and files
            beside each note) or JSON (text, tags, links, attachments and history; enough to rebuild a note).
            Links between notes point at the exported files. <strong>Save package…</strong> writes everything,
            including the trash and history, as one <code>.worriorvex</code> file: a zip you can open anywhere.
            A single note is exported from its right-click menu.
        </p>
        <h3>Privacy</h3>
        <p>
            WorriorVex does not connect to the internet. There is no account, no telemetry and no
            analytics. The two exceptions are both yours to trigger: choosing a link, which opens in your
            browser, and the optional check for a new version (Settings → Updates, off by default), which
            sends one request to github.com asking for the newest version number and nothing else.
        </p>
        <h3>Keeping the data somewhere else</h3>
        <p>
            Start WorriorVex with the environment variable <code>WORRIORVEX_DATA_DIR</code> set to
            another folder and it uses that folder instead.
        </p>
        break;

    default:
        <h2>What is coming</h2>
        <p>WorriorVex is young. Still planned:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Android and iOS</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Follow progress, download new versions or report a problem at
            <ExternalLink Address="AppInfo.Repository">github.com/cloudworrior-labs/worriorvex</ExternalLink>.
            More from the makers at <ExternalLink Address="AppInfo.Website">www.cloudworrior.com</ExternalLink>.
        </p>
        break;
}

